# Registration + Moving Users to Postgres — How the Pieces Fit

> Read this top to bottom. It explains every Spring Boot file involved in two
> things you just built: **(A)** storing users in Postgres instead of memory, and
> **(B)** the `/api/auth/register` endpoint. You are a beginner, so this starts
> from zero and explains *why* each file exists and *how* they link.

---

## ⚠️ SECURITY FLAG (read before anything else) — ANYONE CAN BE ADMIN

Your `/api/auth/register` endpoint accepts the role **from the client**:

```json
POST /api/auth/register
{ "username": "evil", "password": "x", "email": "x@x.com", "role": "ADMIN" }
```

...and the backend stores it **as-is** (`AuthController.register()` →
`user.setRole(request.role())`). There is **no check anywhere** that stops it.

**What this means:** any stranger can open Postman, send one request, and walk
out with a full `ADMIN` account. That account can then do anything an admin
does. This is a *real vulnerability* — not a theoretical one — and it exists
**today** in your code.

**Why the frontend dropdown is NOT a fix:** the frontend only offers
`CUSTOMER`/`SELLER`, but the frontend is just a program sending HTTP. The API
is public and accepts any JSON. Anyone can skip your UI and hit the API
directly. **The backend must be the gatekeeper.**

**The fix (do this before anything else in Phase 1):** in
`AuthController.register()`, reject `ADMIN`:

```java
if (request.role() == Role.ADMIN) {
    return ResponseEntity.status(403).body(Map.of("error", "You cannot sign up as ADMIN"));
}
```

Or, even safer, **ignore the client-supplied role entirely** and always default
to `CUSTOMER` at signup — sellers get upgraded by an existing admin later.
(Keep reading: it's also listed as improvement #3 in section 12.)

---

## 0. The big picture

Think of your app as a restaurant:

| Piece            | Analogy                       | File(s)                                   |
|------------------|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|
| Menu/ingredients | What Spring can cook          | `pom.xml` (dependencies)                  |
| Pantry address   | Where the fridge is           | `application.properties` (DB connection)  |
| Recipe card      | How a person is "described"   | `User.java` + `Role.java`                 |
| Waiter's notepad | The form the guest fills out  | `RegisterRequest.java`                    |
| Kitchen pass     | Who is allowed to order food  | `SecurityConfig.java`                     |
| Counter window   | Where orders arrive           | `AuthController.java`                     |
| Person-finder    | Who looks up an old guest     | `CustomUserDetailsService.java`           |
| Storage room     | The actual database           | Postgres (in DBeaver)                     |

The single most important idea for a beginner:

> **Spring does NOT "know" your database. You connect them with small pieces of
> glue code. Each file is one piece of glue.**

---

## 1. `pom.xml` — "what tools are available?"

Without these two dependencies, none of this works:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
    <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
    <scope>runtime</scope>
</dependency>
```

- **`spring-boot-starter-data-jpa`** = the ORM bundle (JPA + Hibernate). This is
  the thing that can turn a Java class into a database table and back.
- **`postgresql`** = the driver. Java can't "speak Postgres" by itself; this jar
  is the translator between Java and the Postgres protocol.

The other important one (already present) is `spring-boot-starter-security` —
that's what makes `PasswordEncoder`, `UserDetailsService`, etc. exist.

---

## 2. `application.properties` — "where is the database?"

```properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/ecom
spring.datasource.username=postgres
spring.datasource.password=<YOUR POSTGRES PASSWORD>
```

- `jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/ecom` = the address of the fridge:
  protocol `jdbc:postgresql`, host `localhost`, port `5432`, database `ecom`.
- These three lines tell Spring "when you want to talk to a DB, go here."

> ⚠️ **Check before you run:** your file is missing these two lines. Without
> `ddl-auto`, Hibernate will NOT create the `users` table automatically for
> Postgres (Boot only auto-creates tables for embedded DBs like H2):
>
> ```properties
> spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
> spring.jpa.show-sql=true
> ```
>
> `update` = Hibernate creates/alters tables to match your entities (fine while
> learning; **Flyway migrations replace it in Phase 2**). `show-sql` prints the
> generated SQL in the console — extremely educational.

---

## 3. `Role.java` — "which badges exist?"

```java
public enum Role {
    CUSTOMER,
    SELLER,
    ADMIN
}
```

An enum is just a fixed list of allowed values. `User.role` can only be one of
these three. This is the *badge* that decides what a user may do later.

---

## 4. `User.java` — "what is a person, to the database?"

This is the file that turns a Java class into a table. The key annotations:

| Annotation | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `@Entity` | "This Java class = one row of a table." |
| `@Table(name = "users")` | Name the table `users` (`user` is a reserved word in Postgres). |
| `@Id` | This field is the primary key. |
| `@GeneratedValue(strategy = IDENTITY)` | Let the DB auto-increment the id (1, 2, 3...). |
| `@Column(unique = true, nullable = false)` | Username: required, never repeats. DB-level safety net. |
| `@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)` | Store role as text `'SELLER'`, not as a position number. |

So the class `User` maps 1-to-1 to the table `users`, and a `new User()` later
becomes a row in Postgres. The empty constructor `public User() {}` is needed
only by JPA (it creates empty objects, then fills the fields).

**Name clash warning:** your entity is named `User` and Spring Security has its
own `User` class too. That's why you see fully-qualified names like
`org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.User` in the service — to tell
them apart.

---

## 5. `UserRepository.java` — "the auto-magic database helper"

```java
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    Optional<User> findByUserName(String userName);
    boolean existsByUserName(String userName);
}
```

This is an **interface** — no implementation anywhere. Spring Data JPA sees
`extends JpaRepository<User, Long>` and:

1. generates the real class at runtime ("proxy"),
2. registers it as a Spring bean, so other classes can inject it,
3. reads your method *names* and writes the SQL for you:

```
findByUserName   →  SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?
existsByUserName →  SELECT EXISTS(SELECT 1 FROM users WHERE username = ?)
```

`<User, Long>` = "manages the `User` entity, whose primary key is a `Long`."
The name-match is case-insensitive, so `findByUserName` finds the `username`
field.

---

## 6. `RegisterRequest.java` — "the signup form"

```java
public record RegisterRequest(String username, String password, String email, Role role) {
}
```

A **record** = a tiny class that only carries data (auto-generates constructor +
accessors like `username()`, `password()`...). When a client POSTs JSON, Jackson
(inside Spring) fills this record from the request body.

**Why not use `User` directly?** Because they are different shapes with
different rules:
- `RegisterRequest` = what the client is allowed to send (4 safe fields).
- `User` = the database entity (has `id`, DB constraints, JPA annotations).

Accepting a `User` from the internet would let a hacker set `id`, `role=ADMIN`,
etc. **Never accept entities from a request.**

---

## 7. `AuthController.java` — "the counter window"

The only file that actually *talks* to the client. It has three endpoints under
`/api/auth`:

```
POST /api/auth/register   ← the new one (this README)
POST /api/auth/login      ← existing
GET  /api/auth/me         ← existing
```

`register(...)` does 4 jobs:

```java
1. if (userRepository.existsByUserName(request.username())) → 409 "Username already taken"
2. User user = new User();                                  → make an empty person
3. user.setPassword(passwordEncoder.encode(...));           → HASH the password (BCrypt)
4. userRepository.save(user);                               → INSERT into users
   return 201                                               → tell the client "created"
```

The important security line is `passwordEncoder.encode(...)` — the raw password
**never** touches the database. DBeaver shows a `$2a$10$...` hash instead.

---

## 8. `CustomUserDetailsService.java` — "the new person-finder"

This is the file that replaced the old in-memory users. It implements Spring's
`UserDetailsService`, which is just:

> "Who is this username? Give me their `UserDetails`."

```java
@Service
public class CustomUserDetailsService implements UserDetailsService {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String username) {
        User user = userRepository.findByUserName(username)      // 1. ask Postgres
                .orElseThrow(() -> new UsernameNotFoundException("User not found"));
        return org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.User
                .withUsername(user.getUsername())
                .password(user.getPassword())
                .authorities(List.of(new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + user.getRole().name())))
                .build();                                        // 2. make Spring's ID card
    }
}
```

- Before this class, `SecurityConfig` had an `InMemoryUserDetailsManager` bean
  with hardcoded users. **Now it's commented out** (`SecurityConfig.java:43`) —
  this `@Service` is the replacement, backed by Postgres.
- `ROLE_CUSTOMER` / `ROLE_SELLER` / `ROLE_ADMIN` is the "badge" format Spring
  Security expects (the `ROLE_` prefix is required).

---

## 9. `SecurityConfig.java` — "who is allowed in?"

Two things it contributes:

1. **`permitAll()` for registration** — so an anonymous visitor (not yet logged
   in!) can reach `/api/auth/register`. Everything else needs a token:
   ```java
   .requestMatchers("/", "/api/public", "/api/auth/login", "/api/auth/register",
                    "/api/inventory/**", "/api/order/**").permitAll()
   .anyRequest().authenticated()
   ```
2. **The `PasswordEncoder` bean** — BCrypt. This is the exact bean
   `AuthController` injects for `encode()` and login's `matches()`.
   ```java
   @Bean
   public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() { return new BCryptPasswordEncoder(); }
   ```

---

## 10. `JWTAuthFilter` + `JWTService` (login half — brief)

Not needed for registration itself, but relevant to "moving users to DB":

- `JWTService` creates/validates the token (a signed "ID card" with the username
  inside, using `app.jwt.secret` from `application.properties`).
- `JWTAuthFilter` runs on every request: if a `Bearer` token is present, it
  calls `CustomUserDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(...)` to rebuild the
  authenticated user **from Postgres** on every request.

So a freshly registered user can immediately log in and get a token, because
login now goes through the same database-backed person-finder.

---

## 11. How the files LINK (dependency graph)

Read top to bottom; an arrow means "uses":

```
pom.xml ──provides──► spring-boot-starter-data-jpa + postgresql driver
                            │
application.properties ─────┴──► connects to Postgres (db "ecom")
                            │
User.java + Role.java ──────┤  JPA maps these to tables
                            │
UserRepository.java ◄───────┘  generated implementation, injected as bean
       ▲                          ▲
       │ findByUserName()         │ save(), existsByUserName()
       │                          │
CustomUserDetailsService ──► AuthController.register()
       │  (implements                 │  uses
       │   UserDetailsService)        │  passwordEncoder.encode()  [bean from SecurityConfig]
       │                              │
   Spring Security login ◄───────────┘
   (JWTAuthFilter calls the person-finder on every token request)
```

**The complete registration journey:**

```
Angular register form
   │  POST /api/auth/register   (JSON: username, password, email, role)
   ▼
SecurityConfig        → "/api/auth/register" is permitAll(), so no token needed
   ▼
AuthController.register()
   │  1. existsByUserName()?       → UserRepository → SELECT ... FROM users → 409 if exists
   │  2. passwordEncoder.encode()  → BCrypt hash of the password
   │  3. new User() + set fields
   │  4. userRepository.save(user) → Hibernate → INSERT INTO users ... → Postgres
   ▼
201 "User registered successfully"  → back to Angular → redirect to /login
   ▼
(user logs in: loadUserByUsername reads that same row from Postgres, returns
 UserDetails, JWTService mints a token) ✅
```

---

## 12. Suggested improvements (in learning order)

**Must-do before you keep going**

1. **Add `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update`** (see section 2). Without it,
   Postgres gets no `users` table and registration fails with
   `relation "users" does not exist`.
2. **Don't commit the DB password.** Your real Postgres password sits in
   `application.properties`. Use an environment variable:
   ```properties
   spring.datasource.password=${DB_PASSWORD}
   ```
   (Phase 0 wants H2 for dev + Postgres via profiles — this is that moment.)
3. **Block self-registration as ADMIN.** Right now anyone can POST
   `"role": "ADMIN"` and become an admin. This is a genuine vulnerability.
   Simplest fix: in `register(...)`, reject `request.role() == Role.ADMIN`
   with 400/403. A frontend-only fix is not enough.
   **(See the ⚠️ SECURITY FLAG section at the top — fix this first.)**

**Do these when you learn validation (Phase 3)**

4. Add Bean Validation to `RegisterRequest`:
   ```java
   public record RegisterRequest(
       @NotBlank String username,
       @NotBlank @Email String email,
       @NotBlank @Size(min = 8) String password,
       Role role) {}
   ```
   plus `@Valid` on the controller parameter and a global
   `@RestControllerAdvice` for clean 400 responses.
5. Make the username/email unique check **atomic**. `existsByUserName` then
   `save` has a tiny race (two requests could both pass the check). The real
   protection is the DB `unique` constraint (already there) + catching
   `DataIntegrityViolationException`. This is a great story for the Phase 4
   concurrency topic.

**Cleanliness / structure (Phase 2–3)**

6. Move the registration logic out of the controller into an `AuthService`
   (`controller → service → repository` layering).
7. Let Lombok generate the `User` getters/setters (`@Getter @Setter` on the
   class) to kill the boilerplate — only after you understand what they do.
8. Add a seeded ADMIN user on startup (a `CommandLineRunner`) so you always
   have a known admin to log in as. The old `admin`/`password` in-memory users
   are gone now.

**Later / Phase 1 completion**

9. Access + refresh tokens, refresh rotation, and logout (revoke refresh token).
10. Return a **structured JSON error** consistently (e.g. `{"error": "..."}`)
    instead of a plain string — your Angular `error` handling currently guesses
    between the two.
